+## Proposed strict gzip integration
+
+The isolated integration replaces native `DecompressionStream` with the
+reviewed strict, demand-driven single-member gzip reader. It snapshots compressed
+input and cancels the reader synchronously when parsing completes or refuses.
+The decoded ceiling, UTF-8 decoding, JSON parsing, producer limits, lane
+admission, writer selection, and dead-letter protocol are unchanged.
+
+The error priority deliberately changes: once expanded bytes cross the ceiling,
+resource refusal takes precedence over an unread malformed tail. The four
+recorded differences are a bad CRC, a bad ISIZE, a truncated trailer, and a
+trailing zero after an over-budget member. These deliveries retry rather than
+returning null and being acknowledged as malformed. The reader must not finish
+the unread tail merely to recover a later gzip error. This is a proposed
+resource-first contract, not an equivalence claim or deployment authorization.
+
+Wiring this decoder into a production entrypoint would affect every compressed
+queue delivery accepted by `decompressSyncBatchMessage`. Qualification applies
+the integration only in a separate test tree; it does not activate a queue
+route. The main consumer still retains complete JSON text and parsed objects.
+No whole-owner 128 MiB bound, three-scan projected-pipeline result, provider retry
+exhaustion, production retention, or production KV consistency is established.
